## Proof

We wish to compute

$$\beta = \lim_{n\to\infty}\int_{-9}^{44} f_n(x)\,dx,\qquad f_n(x)=2|x|\bigl(1-\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)\bigr).$$

**Step 1 — Pointwise limit.**

For every real $x$, $|\sin(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)|\le 1$, so $\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)\in[0,1]$.

- If $|\sin(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)|<1$, then $\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)\to 0$, hence $f_n(x)\to 2|x|$.
- If $|\sin(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)|=1$, i.e. $\tfrac{\pi}{2}x = \tfrac{\pi}{2}+k\pi$ for some $k\in\mathbb{Z}$, i.e. $x=2k+1$ is an **odd integer**, then $\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)=1$ for all $n$, so $f_n(x)=0$ for all $n$, and $f_n(x)\to 0$.

The set of odd integers in $[-9,44]$ is $\{-9,-7,\dots,43\}$, a finite set, hence of Lebesgue measure zero. Therefore

$$f_n(x)\to 2|x|\quad\text{a.e. on }[-9,44].$$

**Step 2 — Dominating function (applying the Dominated Convergence Theorem).**

For all $n$ and all $x$,

$$0\le 1-\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)\le 1,$$

so

$$0\le f_n(x)=2|x|\bigl(1-\sin^{2n}(\tfrac{\pi}{2}x)\bigr)\le 2|x|.$$

The function $g(x)=2|x|$ is integrable on the bounded interval $[-9,44]$. Since $|f_n|\le g$ with $g\in L^1([-9,44])$, and $f_n\to 2|x|$ a.e., the **Dominated Convergence Theorem** permits interchange of limit and integral:

$$\beta=\int_{-9}^{44}\lim_{n\to\infty}f_n(x)\,dx=\int_{-9}^{44}2|x|\,dx.$$

**Step 3 — Evaluate the integral.**

$$\int_{-9}^{44}2|x|\,dx=2\!\left(\int_{-9}^{0}(-x)\,dx+\int_{0}^{44}x\,dx\right)=2\!\left(\frac{9^{2}}{2}+\frac{44^{2}}{2}\right)=2\cdot\frac{81+1936}{2}=81+1936=2017.$$

**Conclusion.**

$$\boxed{\beta=2017}.$$

